Creating a custom REST service in Dynamics AX involves not only writing a X++ script but also configuring AOT and IIS. The whole process is quite lengthy to be covered in a single response. Here's a simplified version of the X++ class that will represent your service:

```
[DataContractAttribute, SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
public class MyCustomService
{
    str value;

    [DataMemberAttribute("Value")]
    public str parmValue(str _value = value)
    {
        value = _value;
        return value;
    }

    [SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
    public str doSomeWork(str _input)
    {
        // Write you custom code here
        info(strfmt("Work is done with the following input: %1", _input));
        return strfmt("Work is done with the following input: %1", _input);
    }
}
```

The methods marked with `SysEntryPointAttribute(true)` can be exposed as operations in your service.

Please be aware that creating custom services (both SOAP and REST) in Dynamics AX involves multiple steps including creating the appropriate classes in AOT, configuring them as service operations, generating the WSDL document and configuring IIS for service hosting. The above code only demonstrates how to create a X++ class that can be exposed as a service operation. You should look for a detailed step-by-step guide online or consult the Dynamics AX documentation for more information.

